{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>אל נא.</b> Do not, pray, say so to me." "<b>אם נא מצאתי חן בעיניך ולקחת מנחתי מידי כי על כן ראיתי פניך וגו'.</b> For it is worthy and fitting for you to accept my gift, because I saw your face — and it is as precious to me as seeing the face of the angel, for I saw your appointed angel; and further, because you were appeased and forgave my offenses. And why did he mention seeing the angel to him? So that he would fear him and say: He saw angels and was delivered — I cannot prevail against him from now on." "<b>ותרצני.</b> You were appeased to me. And so every רָצוֹן in Scripture is a term of appeasement — אפייצימנ\"טו בְּלַעַ\"ז. As in ״כִּי לֹא לְרָצוֹן יִהְיֶה לָכֶם״ (Leviticus 22) — the offerings come to appease and to propitiate; and likewise ״שִׂפְתֵי צַדִּיק יֵדְעוּן רָצוֹן״ (Proverbs 10) — they know how to appease and to propitiate."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {2 "the la'az is Old French apaisement, 'appeasement'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:01:57.094069141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}